                <pb facs="1039" n="1013" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v16/pg1013"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751G.00/6–354: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The United States
                            Delegation to the Department of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-06-03T15:00:00+01:00">June 3, 1954—3 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Dulte 142. Repeated information Paris 376.
                    Paris limit distribution. I told Eden this
                    morning that with the deteriorating military situation Indochina and the
                    Vietnam-French military talks now going on here I was seriously concerned that
                    if within the next week or two the Communists offered France a cease-fire the
                    French would be in a weak position to resist and might have to accept without
                    any agreement on effective international control machinery or with machinery
                    which would be purely nominal. Eden said
                    he fully shared my fears in this regard.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Smith</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
